Building locally
----------------

The packaging builds one binary package with the service, auth helpers, and
both editor plugins. Build dependencies come from debian/control; gpclient is
a runtime dependency and is not needed for the build or the simulated tests.

On Ubuntu 24.04 or 26.04, from a clean checkout:

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install build-essential devscripts equivs
    sudo mk-build-deps --install --remove \
        --tool 'apt-get --yes --no-install-recommends' debian/control
    dpkg-buildpackage --build=binary --no-sign
    tools/test-deb.sh ../network-manager-gpclient_*.deb

dpkg-buildpackage runs make check and writes the package to the parent
directory. It does not install the plugin or require a running VPN, desktop,
or NetworkManager. Runtime compatibility with a VPN server is a separate check.

GitHub releases
---------------

.github/workflows/deb.yml builds Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) and 26.04 (resolute), on
native amd64 and arm64 runners using the corresponding Ubuntu containers.
Pull requests, pushes to main/master, and manual workflow dispatches build
and validate packages and retain them as workflow artifacts.

To release:

1. Update debian/changelog with the release's Debian version and changes.
   Update AC_INIT in configure.ac when changing the upstream version.
2. Commit and push the changes, and wait for the package workflow to pass.
3. Tag that commit with v followed by the exact Debian version, then push:

       git tag -a v0.1.0-1 -m 'NetworkManager-gpclient 0.1.0-1'
       git push origin v0.1.0-1

4. The workflow checks the tag against debian/changelog, appends a distro
   suffix (for example 0.1.0-1+ubuntu24.04), builds all four packages, and
   creates a draft GitHub release. It attaches the .deb files, a source
   archive of the tagged commit, and SHA256SUMS. No additional secret is
   needed: the release job uses GITHUB_TOKEN with contents: write.
5. Review the draft's files and installation notes, then publish the release.

For packaging-only changes, increment the Debian revision (for example
0.1.0-2). Re-running a tag workflow replaces assets on its existing release;
use a new version once a release has been published.

The first release targets the listed Ubuntu versions. Other Debian-family
distributions can build from this directory, but require their own dependency
and runtime validation. The workflow does not bundle gpclient or configure
the upstream PPA on users' machines. Installation instructions are kept in
debian/release-notes.md and copied into each release description.
